    "body": "At the American Heart Association conference, investigators were supportive of further studying Novo’s IL-6 inhibitor ziltivekimab in patients with less severe cardiovascular and kidney disease.\nNovo’s IL-6 inhibitor ziltivekimab may have a future in treating less sick patients with cardiovascular and chronic kidney disease, despite a Phase 3 failure earlier this year that called the entire drug class into question.\nInvestigators presented follow-up results from the late-stage ZEUS trial at the American Heart Association conference in Chicago over the weekend to a standing-room-only crowd, according to BMO Capital Markets.\nThe study’s population ended up being heavily weighted by a high-risk group of patients with advanced cardiorenal disease who had a higher prevalence of heart failure, the investigators argued. This “raises the possibility that the mechanism was appropriate, but the population was not,” BMO wrote on Sunday.\nZiltivekimab appeared to work as intended by reducing inflammation, with an associated reduction in cholesterol. But the trial did not meet its main goal of reducing the risk of major cardiovascular events as compared to placebo.\n“ZEUS reinforces that neither elevated inflammatory biomarkers nor their substantial reduction guarantees benefit across populations,” BMO wrote.\nImmunology and inflammation\nNovo’s late-stage IL-6 stumble shakes inflammation space\nNovo Nordisk was banking on the inflammatory benefits of its investigational therapy ziltivekimab to improve cardiovascular outcomes. Last week’s Phase 3 failure dashed not just the Danish pharma’s hopes but that of other drugmakers working on the same pathway.\nAugust 3, 2026\n·\n2 min read\n·\nTristan Manalac\nRead more\nTo advance ziltivekimab, Novo should focus on patient selection, the investigators said. They were generally open to further testing of the drug, supported by studies of similar therapies in the class such as Novartis’ successful Phase 3 CANTOS trial of canakinumab in 2017.\nAfter the ZEUS failure, Novo ended several studies of ziltivekimab, including the Phase 3 HERMES and ATHENA tests. But the Phase 3 ARTEMIS trial in the acute setting is continuing on, featuring patients who have had a heart attack. The investigators noted that patients enrolled in ARTEMIS have less chronic kidney disease and heart failure as compared to ZEUS’ population, according to BMO. ZEUS’ failure sent shockwaves across the inflammatory disease space this summer, with shares tumbling for companies like BioAge Labs and Monte Rosa Therapeutics, which are developing drugs to reduce cardiovascular risk by lowering inflammation.\n“The ZEUS data do not refute the broader cardiovascular inflammation hypothesis (for which there is already positive data),” BMO said.\nThe AHA presentation makes a case for continuing to explore inflammation reduction as a means of addressing cardiovascular disease.\ncardiovascular disease\nNovo shutters 2 more heart disease studies, indicating IL-6 is ‘invalid target’\nAfter the failure of Novo Nordisk’s IL-6 inhibitor ziltivekimab sent waves through the industry, the company has terminated two other studies of the asset, bringing the total number of high-profile cardiovascular flops to six over nine weeks.\nSeptember 8, 2026\n·\n2 min read\n·\nGabrielle Masson\nRead more",
